WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.040
Containment limits immediate harm.

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:07.060
Short-term containment may restrict an identity, block suspicious network paths,

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:13.600
revoke credentials, isolate workloads, disable exposed access, or pause risky automation.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.380
Long-term containment addresses the weakness that allowed the incident to persist,

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:24.200
such as overbroad permissions, weak logging, exposed storage, unsafe deployment defaults,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.180
or missing guardrails.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.240
Containment should be scoped carefully.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:34.060
Overly broad actions can cause outages, erase evidence, or affect unrelated systems.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.080
Under-scoped actions can leave active risk in place.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.220
Responders should document the reason for each action, the expected effect,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.480
the owner who approved it, and any business impact.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.020
Good containment is controlled, observable, and reversible where possible.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:54.120
Eradication removes unauthorized resources, credentials, configuration,

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.140
persistence mechanisms, or exposed paths.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.680
In cloud environments, eradication may include removing unauthorized keys,

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.380
deleting unapproved resources after evidence is preserved, correcting policies,

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.680
replacing compromised workloads,

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.580
and redeploying from known-good images or infrastructure-as-code.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.160
It should be paired with monitoring to confirm the activity has stopped.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.700
Recovery restores trusted service.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.400
That may involve rebuilding workloads, restoring data from known-good backups,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:28.260
validating configuration, redeploying infrastructure, re-enabling access,

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.640
and confirming monitoring coverage.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:32.620
Recovery sequencing matters.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.880
If identities are restored before policies are fixed,

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.460
or workloads are rebuilt before exposed storage is secured,

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.520
the environment may return to risk.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.460
Avoid rushed changes that create new incidents.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:48.680
A responder under pressure may want to delete, rebuild, rotate,

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.060
and reconfigure everything at once.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.440
Sometimes urgent action is necessary,

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:57.020
but uncontrolled changes can make impact harder to understand.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.600
A strong recovery plan restores service while preserving evidence, validating controls,

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.660
and watching for recurrence.
